WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.437
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.737 --> 00:00:06.320
INDRANIL CHAKRABORTY:
Hello, everyone.

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:10.210
Thank you for coming to
this session of IoT solution

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:11.740
on Google Cloud.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:15.010
I am Indranil Chakraborty,
I'm a product guy

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:18.700
at Google Cloud working on IoT.

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:22.460
What we're going to cover
today is three things.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:25.840
One is how do we
think about security

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:28.840
when it comes to building
IoT solution on Cloud?

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:32.350
Second, how do we handle
scale when we think

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:34.730
about IoT solution on Cloud?

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:37.980
And finally, how do we
get actionable insight

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:40.060
for when we build IoT solution?

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:43.540
We'll also have a demo at the
end where one of our partner

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:47.590
has built a pretty
interesting solution for IoT

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:49.750
on Google Cloud.

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:52.890
Let's get started.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:55.080
What is IoT?

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:58.440
If you Google
internet of things,

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:02.190
one of the first simple
definition that you see

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:08.220
is interconnect via internet
of computing devices embedded

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.020
in everyday things.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:13.530
Essentially, everyday
things interconnected

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:14.640
via the internet.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.440
Hence, internet of things.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:21.090
And one of the main application
is the first application

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:25.230
you see of internet
of things is at home.

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:32.560
When you think about
Nest thermostat,

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:35.590
you don't have to turn
on your thermostat,

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:37.930
or you don't have to
turn off your thermostat

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:39.370
when you leave the home.

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:41.860
The Nest thermostat
has a sensor.

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:44.500
It knows when you come
inside your house,

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:48.502
and it turns on the heater
to a set temperature.

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:56.290
Google Home can also interact
with your Nest thermostat.

00:01:56.290 --> 00:01:59.620
It can even turn on
or off your light.

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:02.680
In fact, you can
use Nest thermostat

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:06.360
to turn off the light
when you leave the home.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:10.889
So simple everyday
things interacting

00:02:10.889 --> 00:02:15.730
with each other via the internet
to create simple magic at home.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:21.750
And when you think about
internet of things,

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:25.740
the application of
IoT is much broader

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:28.540
outside the context of home.

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:32.190
Let's take example
the parking spaces.

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:35.700
It is estimated that there
are around 800 million parking

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:38.230
spaces in the United States.

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:42.910
San Francisco city alone
has close to half a million

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:45.960
public parking spaces,
but it's still really hard

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.460
to find parking in this area.

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:50.790
In fact the San
Francisco city has

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:55.740
done an interesting
experiment with SF Park where

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.230
they have embittered
small sensors

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:01.620
in a number of public
spaces which can accurately

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:06.240
detect whether a parking
space is occupied or not.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:12.940
And this information is sent
to Cloud via the internet.

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:15.740
And so imagine if
you are driving

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:17.870
to meet your friend
at a restaurant,

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:21.890
your car knows exactly where
to find a parking space

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:25.490
because your car is interfacing
via internet with all

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:28.610
the parking space which in
turn is sending real time

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:32.890
update about the availability
of parking space to your car.

00:03:32.890 --> 00:03:35.600
Again, everyday
things interacting

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:40.160
with each other via internet
to really make our life easier.

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:43.310
It will also be
nice if while you

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:47.380
are with your
friend at the cafe,

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:52.520
you get an alert about as the
meter is running expiring,

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:55.730
and so you can pay or extend the
meter right from on your phone.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:02.120
We also seeing a lot of
interesting application

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:04.790
in the industrial
space, as well.

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:08.060
In fact, we have
partners and customers

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:11.390
who are working with
manufacturing industries

00:04:11.390 --> 00:04:17.510
where they're able
to remotely monitor

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:21.170
a lot of their machines
across multiple factories.

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:25.820
You see, many of these machines
already collect a lot of data,

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:27.150
even today.

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:29.810
But unfortunately, they're
not able to use it.

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:33.300
Less than 10% of all the
data is actually put to use.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:36.590
So imagine if all these
machines are connected

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:39.770
over the internet, and as
an Enterprise customer,

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:45.350
or as a factory owner, you
can monitor all your machines

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:48.260
remotely across
different regions,

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:51.120
or even across the globe.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:54.290
So we see that there's
a lot of applications

00:04:54.290 --> 00:04:58.520
of internet of things when it
comes to industrial segment,

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:01.240
as well.

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:07.290
In fact, at Google, we think
internet of things or IoT,

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:11.010
as we call it, is at
a point of inflection.

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:14.970
Depending on the analyst
report you look into,

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:18.930
it is estimated that
by 2020 you will

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:23.700
have anywhere from 20
billion to 40 billion devices

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:25.690
connected to internet.

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:31.020
Now, each of these small devices
might send a few bytes of data

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:32.490
every second.

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:36.120
But when you look at
it in a cumulative way,

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:38.190
the overall amount
of data of which

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:41.940
these devices will generate
is going to be massive.

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:45.900
And so it creates three
challenges for developers,

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:48.740
for partners, when they
start thinking about building

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:50.790
IoT solution on Cloud.

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:54.450
One is security.

00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:57.600
And the question
about security is,

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:00.900
how do we make sure that
none of the devices which

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:04.800
are out on the field
connected is compromised?

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:08.250
The recent Dyn attack
is a staunch reminder

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:10.710
that there is a lot of
vulnerability out there.

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:14.700
And how do we make sure that
the data which these devices are

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:19.590
generating is secured not just
when it's stored in the Cloud,

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:23.040
but also during transit as
it's transiting from the device

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:23.610
to the Cloud?

00:06:26.230 --> 00:06:29.900
And the second
challenge is scale.

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:33.140
When you have so many millions
or billions of devices,

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:35.990
how do you make sure
that all those devices

00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:39.560
is running the same version of
operating system, same version

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:42.830
of firmware, has the
same security patch

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:44.990
so it's very hard to compromise?

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:50.720
And then how do you make sure
that the torrent of data which

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:53.120
would be generated
by so many devices

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:56.750
can be easily handled by
your application on the Cloud

00:06:56.750 --> 00:06:58.930
or on-prem?

00:06:58.930 --> 00:07:02.170
And finally, insights.

00:07:02.170 --> 00:07:05.890
Well, you can have a torrent
of data from all these devices,

00:07:05.890 --> 00:07:08.860
but what do you
do with this data?

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:13.510
How do you tease out really
interesting, actionable insight

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:16.940
from the data, which you can
then apply on your application

00:07:16.940 --> 00:07:18.130
or for your business case?

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:25.550
At Google, we have
been thinking hard

00:07:25.550 --> 00:07:29.820
about these challenges
for a number of years.

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:33.680
And in fact, when it comes
to massive amount of data

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:37.370
processing, ingesting,
and then analyzing,

00:07:37.370 --> 00:07:39.860
we've been working
on big data problems

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:43.350
for over a decade
in Google Cloud.

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:48.900
So we have Pub/Sub, Data
Flow Bigtable, Cloud Email,

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:53.990
to name a few of the services
which can operate at scale

00:07:53.990 --> 00:07:56.160
and handle massive
amount of data,

00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:58.310
which I will talk more about.

00:07:58.310 --> 00:08:01.220
When it comes to
internet of things,

00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:04.700
we not only think in
the context of Cloud

00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:08.450
for security,
scale, and insight,

00:08:08.450 --> 00:08:12.620
but we also think about how do
we have the same at the Edge

00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:14.320
as well?

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:19.700
And so it is my pleasure to
invite Venkat Rapaka, who

00:08:19.700 --> 00:08:21.260
is director of
product manager who

00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:23.840
leads IoT platform
on Google to talk

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:28.470
about how we think about Edge
devices and Android things.

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:28.970
Venkat.

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:37.998
VENKAT RAPAKA: Thanks, Indranil.

00:08:37.998 --> 00:08:38.539
Hi, everyone.

00:08:38.539 --> 00:08:41.336
I'm Venkat Rapaka, as
Indranil mentioned.

00:08:41.336 --> 00:08:42.710
I want to talk a
little bit today

00:08:42.710 --> 00:08:48.500
about how the notions of scale
security and insight, obviously

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:52.610
they're as important on the
Edge as on the Cloud side,

00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:54.826
and I'm excited to
talk about our latest

00:08:54.826 --> 00:08:57.200
offering in this space that
should be able to deliver all

00:08:57.200 --> 00:08:59.324
of this at the Edge as well.

00:08:59.324 --> 00:09:00.870
I'll talk about Android things.

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:02.830
Let me first talk
about what it is.

00:09:02.830 --> 00:09:05.114
It is Android,
essentially, right?

00:09:05.114 --> 00:09:07.280
Android is one of the most
popular operating systems

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:08.360
out there today.

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:12.360
But it's Android, but
purpose built for IoT.

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:15.070
Think about the internet
of things devices,

00:09:15.070 --> 00:09:17.600
there's some pretty interesting
characteristics to them.

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:19.940
A majority of them
don't have a display.

00:09:19.940 --> 00:09:23.610
When they do have a display,
it depicts purpose devices

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:24.110
typically.

00:09:24.110 --> 00:09:26.845
There is no user-installable
applications, right?

00:09:26.845 --> 00:09:28.220
There's no notion
of the use that

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:31.580
interacting with it to install
new capabilities on the device.

00:09:31.580 --> 00:09:34.340
So what we did is we
removed a number of things

00:09:34.340 --> 00:09:37.145
that we didn't think were
essential in the IoT space.

00:09:37.145 --> 00:09:39.020
Things like the applications
that are Android

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:41.570
comes with,
notification support,

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:45.260
and a bunch of things
that are for UI, similarly

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:51.020
to render a UI with
installable applications.

00:09:51.020 --> 00:09:53.440
And instead replace it
with things that are,

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:55.270
I think, very relevant for IoT.

00:09:55.270 --> 00:09:57.780
Like peripheral support
is a good example.

00:09:57.780 --> 00:09:59.930
So you can think of the
right type of sensors

00:09:59.930 --> 00:10:02.360
that people will be
building into these devices.

00:10:02.360 --> 00:10:05.900
Actuators if you want to
actually operate equipment.

00:10:05.900 --> 00:10:09.500
So we added back
some other things

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:12.110
that we think are very
important to the IoT space,

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:14.060
but in the process
we shrunk roughly

00:10:14.060 --> 00:10:16.934
the footprint of Android
to about half the size.

00:10:16.934 --> 00:10:18.350
Which basically
means that this is

00:10:18.350 --> 00:10:20.870
going to be extensible
to a lot more devices.

00:10:20.870 --> 00:10:23.710
Still, we're talking
about, to be very clear,

00:10:23.710 --> 00:10:25.140
SoC class devices.

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:28.100
These are devices that typically
run something like Linux,

00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:30.170
and they tend to be
line powered devices.

00:10:30.170 --> 00:10:32.295
But these are the types of
devices that you usually

00:10:32.295 --> 00:10:34.650
expect to see at the
edge of the network,

00:10:34.650 --> 00:10:39.100
and that run with a lot
of intelligence on it.

00:10:39.100 --> 00:10:41.950
So I'll talk about Android
things a little bit more

00:10:41.950 --> 00:10:45.340
like, it's an interim solution,
the multiple elements to it.

00:10:45.340 --> 00:10:47.670
So I talked about the
operating system a little bit.

00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:50.440
It's based on
Android, it's secure.

00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:52.640
Android comes with
monthly security updates.

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:55.570
We are committing to multiple
years of monthly security

00:10:55.570 --> 00:10:56.580
updates here.

00:10:56.580 --> 00:10:58.790
It's, of course, updateable.

00:10:58.790 --> 00:11:02.140
I talked about removing a
number of things from Android,

00:11:02.140 --> 00:11:06.340
but the thing that preserved is
a majority of the Android APIs.

00:11:06.340 --> 00:11:09.340
All the APIs that are delivering
for these types of devices

00:11:09.340 --> 00:11:10.670
are all preserved.

00:11:10.670 --> 00:11:13.240
And what that means is
we get all the Android

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:15.220
APIs, and the
developer environment

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:18.070
and the test tools that
are currently available,

00:11:18.070 --> 00:11:21.220
but also a million Android
app developers that exist

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:26.140
today could all become IoT
device if they so choose.

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:29.640
So if you're out there and
you want to build a device,

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:31.960
you no longer need
deep OS expertise.

00:11:31.960 --> 00:11:33.460
You don't need
people who understand

00:11:33.460 --> 00:11:37.150
firmware, and kernel,
and OS level security.

00:11:37.150 --> 00:11:39.070
You should be able to
take Android things

00:11:39.070 --> 00:11:41.950
and be able to
build a device just

00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:45.100
by building an Android app
that can run on top of Android.

00:11:45.100 --> 00:11:46.590
The other thing
that we're doing is

00:11:46.590 --> 00:11:50.830
we're including APIs to
enable these devices to be

00:11:50.830 --> 00:11:52.310
managed at scale.

00:11:52.310 --> 00:11:54.100
So if you have
hundreds or thousands

00:11:54.100 --> 00:11:56.660
of these devices in
your installation,

00:11:56.660 --> 00:11:59.950
you should be able to use a
standard off the shelf console

00:11:59.950 --> 00:12:03.550
and be able to manage
all of these devices.

00:12:03.550 --> 00:12:05.770
But the OS is only
a part of what it

00:12:05.770 --> 00:12:08.710
takes to build a great device.

00:12:08.710 --> 00:12:10.060
There's hardware, right?

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:12.310
And building hardware is
incredibly complicated

00:12:12.310 --> 00:12:13.667
for a lot of people.

00:12:13.667 --> 00:12:15.250
How do you pick the
components, how do

00:12:15.250 --> 00:12:17.180
you assemble them all together?

00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:19.060
And to do that, we are
working very closely

00:12:19.060 --> 00:12:22.060
with a number of major
industry players,

00:12:22.060 --> 00:12:24.370
and we're building with
them pre-certified boards.

00:12:24.370 --> 00:12:26.830
And these boards
come with compute,

00:12:26.830 --> 00:12:29.410
they come with
connectivity and storage.

00:12:29.410 --> 00:12:34.990
So what this does is it takes
both a lot of the complexity,

00:12:34.990 --> 00:12:37.360
and the guesswork,
and the risk that it

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:41.440
takes to build a great device,
but also it takes out the time

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:42.290
to market.

00:12:42.290 --> 00:12:44.350
So these certified
boards, they guarantee

00:12:44.350 --> 00:12:46.060
to run Android things.

00:12:46.060 --> 00:12:48.220
They get regular
updates every month,

00:12:48.220 --> 00:12:51.090
and they get you
started out of the gate

00:12:51.090 --> 00:12:54.440
without having to know a lot
about how to build a device.

00:12:54.440 --> 00:12:58.427
And again, our attempt with
these and with our partners

00:12:58.427 --> 00:13:00.010
is that if you're
trying to prototype,

00:13:00.010 --> 00:13:01.926
you should be able to
buy one of these things,

00:13:01.926 --> 00:13:03.989
not have to commit to
10,000 of them just

00:13:03.989 --> 00:13:05.530
to get to some
minimum order quantity

00:13:05.530 --> 00:13:07.660
to get scale efficiency.

00:13:07.660 --> 00:13:10.136
So the cost will scale
linearly, as well.

00:13:10.136 --> 00:13:12.760
So these are all the ways we're
trying to take the friction out

00:13:12.760 --> 00:13:16.070
of the ability to
build a great device.

00:13:16.070 --> 00:13:17.860
The third thing that's
really interesting

00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:20.200
is that as we're
building this OS,

00:13:20.200 --> 00:13:22.300
we're also looking at
all the types of services

00:13:22.300 --> 00:13:24.880
that we can include
in a turnkey fashion

00:13:24.880 --> 00:13:28.430
to make it easy to build a great
product at the end of the day.

00:13:28.430 --> 00:13:30.640
So a few examples up here.

00:13:30.640 --> 00:13:32.750
The first symbol that
you see is, of course,

00:13:32.750 --> 00:13:33.862
the Google Cloud platform.

00:13:33.862 --> 00:13:35.320
So if you're building
a device that

00:13:35.320 --> 00:13:39.160
requires data to be connected,
like sent back to a cloud,

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:40.850
you should be able
to use Google Cloud

00:13:40.850 --> 00:13:43.330
platform in a seamless way.

00:13:43.330 --> 00:13:45.520
The second one is TensorFlow.

00:13:45.520 --> 00:13:49.090
To be able to do on-device
intelligence, of course.

00:13:49.090 --> 00:13:52.660
Location services,
location services

00:13:52.660 --> 00:13:54.560
come for free with Android.

00:13:54.560 --> 00:13:56.590
The fourth one
symbolizes connectivity.

00:13:56.590 --> 00:13:59.300
Of course, there's Wi-Fi and
Bluetooth as you would expect,

00:13:59.300 --> 00:14:01.450
but also because it's
based on Android,

00:14:01.450 --> 00:14:05.080
you get cellular connectivity
capability as well.

00:14:05.080 --> 00:14:07.240
And this is, of course,
one of the most commonly

00:14:07.240 --> 00:14:11.290
used operating systems by all
the carriers in the world.

00:14:11.290 --> 00:14:14.980
So to be able to build now an
LTE capable device suddenly

00:14:14.980 --> 00:14:17.800
becomes much more accessible
to people using something

00:14:17.800 --> 00:14:19.010
like Android Things.

00:14:19.010 --> 00:14:21.370
And over time, we intend
to support additional types

00:14:21.370 --> 00:14:25.420
of connectivity matters
like thread, for example,

00:14:25.420 --> 00:14:28.570
for local low power
connectivity with devices

00:14:28.570 --> 00:14:31.210
that are out there that are
battery powered, for example.

00:14:31.210 --> 00:14:36.490
So this is the sum total of
what makes Android Things what

00:14:36.490 --> 00:14:39.390
it is, which is an end to
end solution for people

00:14:39.390 --> 00:14:42.580
to be able to build
a great device.

00:14:42.580 --> 00:14:45.730
Let me talk about what
this starts to enable.

00:14:45.730 --> 00:14:47.500
And I'll pick one
specific example

00:14:47.500 --> 00:14:50.020
to talk about, a smart camera.

00:14:50.020 --> 00:14:52.960
So imagine you're trying to
build something like a Nest

00:14:52.960 --> 00:14:55.130
cam, it's a security camera.

00:14:55.130 --> 00:14:59.860
You should be able to
pick from among options

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:01.220
of certified hardware.

00:15:01.220 --> 00:15:04.390
Something that meets your
price and your performance

00:15:04.390 --> 00:15:06.540
characteristics of the
device you want to build.

00:15:06.540 --> 00:15:08.052
Should be able to
get one of those.

00:15:08.052 --> 00:15:09.760
Should be able to
download Android Things

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:12.390
so you have a secure operating
system to go with it.

00:15:12.390 --> 00:15:13.914
But it's a camera
you're building,

00:15:13.914 --> 00:15:15.580
there's a video feed
coming out, and you

00:15:15.580 --> 00:15:17.910
might want to save it
somewhere on the Cloud.

00:15:17.910 --> 00:15:19.510
Or you might want
to be able to access

00:15:19.510 --> 00:15:22.840
it using either a browser
or a mobile device.

00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:26.020
And I mentioned, of course,
the Google Cloud Platform

00:15:26.020 --> 00:15:28.610
and, it's built right in.

00:15:28.610 --> 00:15:30.850
But also bandwidth is expensive.

00:15:30.850 --> 00:15:32.830
And if you have installation
of these devices,

00:15:32.830 --> 00:15:35.050
and you don't want to be
pumping data out all the time.

00:15:35.050 --> 00:15:36.508
You want to be able
to do something

00:15:36.508 --> 00:15:38.730
intelligent on the device
itself to make sure

00:15:38.730 --> 00:15:41.640
that you only send data up to
the Cloud when you want to.

00:15:41.640 --> 00:15:43.590
And for that we have
TensorFlow, right?

00:15:43.590 --> 00:15:46.290
So as an example, you
should be able to look

00:15:46.290 --> 00:15:50.010
at what the camera is
seeing on the device itself

00:15:50.010 --> 00:15:52.500
and decide, what is it
that I'm looking at?

00:15:52.500 --> 00:15:54.150
Is it a cat running
through the yard,

00:15:54.150 --> 00:15:56.010
or is it a person walking up?

00:15:56.010 --> 00:15:57.840
And is it a person
that I trust and know,

00:15:57.840 --> 00:15:59.340
or is it someone
that I don't know,

00:15:59.340 --> 00:16:00.800
and therefore I'm
going to alert?

00:16:00.800 --> 00:16:04.030
So suddenly you have
the variety of options,

00:16:04.030 --> 00:16:07.230
starting all the way
from doing nothing,

00:16:07.230 --> 00:16:10.450
log it but don't wake
somebody up, wake somebody up.

00:16:10.450 --> 00:16:12.630
So you suddenly start
to do all of that

00:16:12.630 --> 00:16:15.030
locally without having
to send a ton of data

00:16:15.030 --> 00:16:17.340
out into the cloud to do that.

00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:21.242
And I talked about one specific
example of a security camera,

00:16:21.242 --> 00:16:22.950
but the thing that
makes this interesting

00:16:22.950 --> 00:16:29.040
is this should be the
same kind of a process.

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:31.770
Just by learning a
different TensorFlow model,

00:16:31.770 --> 00:16:33.360
suddenly you have
a traffic camera

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:37.290
that can figure out the license
plate number on device itself.

00:16:37.290 --> 00:16:40.140
And take a photo of somebody
who violates a red light

00:16:40.140 --> 00:16:43.020
and then sends that photo out.

00:16:43.020 --> 00:16:46.200
You can imagine this camera
sitting inside a building

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:48.360
and doing occupancy
detection and saying,

00:16:48.360 --> 00:16:49.950
this is the number
of people who use

00:16:49.950 --> 00:16:52.200
this space at this
time of the day,

00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:54.240
and only send those
heuristics up, as opposed

00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:55.870
to actually sending
the full data

00:16:55.870 --> 00:16:58.290
feed to have to be
processed on the cloud.

00:16:58.290 --> 00:17:00.184
But all of a sudden,
you can imagine

00:17:00.184 --> 00:17:01.350
building a device like this.

00:17:01.350 --> 00:17:03.520
And just by what
you download to it,

00:17:03.520 --> 00:17:05.560
it suddenly changes
in characteristics

00:17:05.560 --> 00:17:07.829
and what it can do.

00:17:07.829 --> 00:17:10.540
I'm going to turn this
around now and start

00:17:10.540 --> 00:17:13.020
talking about how as
a device developer

00:17:13.020 --> 00:17:14.420
you can build this very easily.

00:17:14.420 --> 00:17:16.170
But I'm going to turn
this around and say,

00:17:16.170 --> 00:17:17.520
let's imagine that
you are an enterprise,

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:19.530
and you have 1,000 of
these cameras sitting

00:17:19.530 --> 00:17:22.050
all around your
buildings worldwide,

00:17:22.050 --> 00:17:26.539
and you want to do
premise monitoring.

00:17:26.539 --> 00:17:28.690
I talked about the
management at scale.

00:17:28.690 --> 00:17:31.680
So you should be able to look
at all of these cameras on one

00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:34.470
console, and you should
be able to update

00:17:34.470 --> 00:17:38.460
the model that you use on it
to maybe do facial recognition.

00:17:38.460 --> 00:17:41.910
Maybe your employee
database changes every day,

00:17:41.910 --> 00:17:44.070
because somebody new
joins, somebody left.

00:17:44.070 --> 00:17:46.500
Maybe you want to download
that new database down

00:17:46.500 --> 00:17:48.920
to each of these cameras
so they can do detection

00:17:48.920 --> 00:17:51.520
at the edge itself for you.

00:17:51.520 --> 00:17:54.540
These are all possible from
a console with one click.

00:17:54.540 --> 00:17:57.719
And the other cool thing is,
you might have cameras also

00:17:57.719 --> 00:17:59.760
running Android Things
inside your building doing

00:17:59.760 --> 00:18:02.010
your occupancy detection,
and your cameras that

00:18:02.010 --> 00:18:03.434
are doing premise monitoring.

00:18:03.434 --> 00:18:05.100
Now they're all based
on Android Things.

00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:07.183
You should be able to use
the same console to look

00:18:07.183 --> 00:18:10.830
at all of them in one spot,
which is exactly how we're

00:18:10.830 --> 00:18:13.752
trying to take the friction
out of an enterprise with all

00:18:13.752 --> 00:18:15.460
the management overhead
you would expect.

00:18:18.260 --> 00:18:19.400
So a lot more happening.

00:18:19.400 --> 00:18:21.789
We're right now in developer
preview with Android Things.

00:18:21.789 --> 00:18:23.330
Over the next few
months, we're going

00:18:23.330 --> 00:18:26.810
to have multiple developer
preview releases coming out.

00:18:26.810 --> 00:18:30.210
Towards the early part of
the second half of this year,

00:18:30.210 --> 00:18:32.900
we're going to go into
commercial availability.

00:18:32.900 --> 00:18:35.220
So please stay tuned,
you'll find more information

00:18:35.220 --> 00:18:36.824
at the tooling subdev.

00:18:36.824 --> 00:18:37.490
Thank you again.

00:18:46.544 --> 00:18:48.210
INDRANIL CHAKRABORTY:
Thank you, Venkat.

00:18:54.520 --> 00:18:58.160
So as Venkat mentioned,
with Android Things,

00:18:58.160 --> 00:19:02.390
you can really build a device
application which is secured,

00:19:02.390 --> 00:19:08.180
which can scale, and you can
use TensorFlow to get insights

00:19:08.180 --> 00:19:10.650
on the edge itself.

00:19:10.650 --> 00:19:15.440
Now let's talk about
Cloud, and how do we

00:19:15.440 --> 00:19:20.420
handle security scale and
insight within Google Cloud?

00:19:20.420 --> 00:19:23.460
There are a number of services
which I've listed here.

00:19:23.460 --> 00:19:26.570
I'm going to talk about a
couple of key services which

00:19:26.570 --> 00:19:29.480
can really help in building
a secure, scalable,

00:19:29.480 --> 00:19:33.370
and insightful application
when it comes to IoT.

00:19:33.370 --> 00:19:36.690
First, let's talk
about security.

00:19:36.690 --> 00:19:42.330
On Google Cloud, every data
by default is encrypted.

00:19:42.330 --> 00:19:44.700
It's encrypted at
rest as well as it's

00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:47.860
encrypted when in transit.

00:19:47.860 --> 00:19:51.150
Now, we understand
that there could

00:19:51.150 --> 00:19:55.530
be a couple of very specific
customer client needs, or user

00:19:55.530 --> 00:19:59.220
needs, because of which you
would need multiple option

00:19:59.220 --> 00:20:01.100
depending on the
level of protection

00:20:01.100 --> 00:20:04.840
that the customer or you
are more comfortable with.

00:20:04.840 --> 00:20:09.150
So we do offer a wide
spectrum of options

00:20:09.150 --> 00:20:11.760
when it comes to security.

00:20:11.760 --> 00:20:15.850
The first, as I
mentioned, is encryption

00:20:15.850 --> 00:20:20.410
by default. In which case you
are using Google Cloud scale

00:20:20.410 --> 00:20:23.830
and velocity and our
key management system

00:20:23.830 --> 00:20:27.970
without needing to
implement any of your own.

00:20:27.970 --> 00:20:31.960
In this case, you would be
using the same key management

00:20:31.960 --> 00:20:34.420
system which is
used by other Google

00:20:34.420 --> 00:20:38.860
products such as Apps, Google
Maps, Search, and others.

00:20:38.860 --> 00:20:41.710
And so you get the
benefit of Google scale

00:20:41.710 --> 00:20:43.000
and infrastructure.

00:20:43.000 --> 00:20:46.480
At the same time, you get
a high degree of security

00:20:46.480 --> 00:20:48.240
when it comes to IoT data.

00:20:50.770 --> 00:20:57.910
For reasons if you do not
choose to trust with us for data

00:20:57.910 --> 00:21:01.720
key management, we
do offer customer

00:21:01.720 --> 00:21:05.140
supplied encryption keys.

00:21:05.140 --> 00:21:07.510
If you would like to
maintain your keys

00:21:07.510 --> 00:21:10.180
and manage your
keys on-prem, you

00:21:10.180 --> 00:21:12.610
could do that with this option.

00:21:12.610 --> 00:21:16.720
In this option, you will
have full control on creation

00:21:16.720 --> 00:21:20.980
and management of keys, and
Google will use those keys only

00:21:20.980 --> 00:21:25.660
at the time of
encryption, but will not

00:21:25.660 --> 00:21:28.140
retain it on the Cloud.

00:21:28.140 --> 00:21:30.830
You will get the
security, but the user

00:21:30.830 --> 00:21:33.325
will have to make sure that
the keys are provisioned

00:21:33.325 --> 00:21:36.770
and managed on-prem.

00:21:36.770 --> 00:21:38.900
We are also working with
customers and partners,

00:21:38.900 --> 00:21:41.930
and we realized that we need to
offer a middle ground, as well.

00:21:45.480 --> 00:21:49.990
If you don't trust us with
the key management system,

00:21:49.990 --> 00:21:52.880
but you don't want
to have full control,

00:21:52.880 --> 00:21:56.070
then we have a middle ground
which is called the Cloud Key

00:21:56.070 --> 00:21:57.360
Management System.

00:21:57.360 --> 00:22:02.860
In this case, you have full
control for key creation,

00:22:02.860 --> 00:22:05.980
deletion, rotation, revocation.

00:22:05.980 --> 00:22:07.950
But the entire key
management system

00:22:07.950 --> 00:22:10.620
is done on Google
Cloud infrastructure.

00:22:10.620 --> 00:22:14.730
So you get the benefit of Google
Cloud, the velocity of Google

00:22:14.730 --> 00:22:16.470
Cloud infrastructure,
but you have

00:22:16.470 --> 00:22:21.750
full control of how you want
to manage your own keys.

00:22:21.750 --> 00:22:26.010
So again, essentially
we offer a high degree

00:22:26.010 --> 00:22:31.440
of security on Cloud with the
option which meets your needs

00:22:31.440 --> 00:22:34.120
and is flexible, as well.

00:22:34.120 --> 00:22:36.310
So that's how we
look at security,

00:22:36.310 --> 00:22:38.700
which is high security,
but at the same time

00:22:38.700 --> 00:22:42.780
we do give you options to
meet your own customer or user

00:22:42.780 --> 00:22:43.280
goals.

00:22:47.100 --> 00:22:50.050
When it comes to scale,
as I mentioned before,

00:22:50.050 --> 00:22:53.190
we have been working on
this for quite a long time.

00:22:53.190 --> 00:22:59.880
And if you take the example
of the parking space,

00:22:59.880 --> 00:23:03.870
individual sensors might be
sending small trickle of data.

00:23:03.870 --> 00:23:06.420
But in aggregate,
when you think about

00:23:06.420 --> 00:23:10.410
even if you have 10 million
more parking spaces in US which

00:23:10.410 --> 00:23:13.290
are all connected with
sensors to indicate

00:23:13.290 --> 00:23:15.450
whether the space
is occupied or not,

00:23:15.450 --> 00:23:17.460
that's a huge volume of data.

00:23:17.460 --> 00:23:19.740
Even if those sensors
are sending the data

00:23:19.740 --> 00:23:22.470
over a few seconds
or a few minutes.

00:23:22.470 --> 00:23:25.770
So you need a service which
can ingest this massive scale

00:23:25.770 --> 00:23:28.160
of data, and then
route this data

00:23:28.160 --> 00:23:32.170
to different services for
processing and analysis.

00:23:32.170 --> 00:23:35.850
And Cloud Pub/Sub is a
great service for that.

00:23:35.850 --> 00:23:38.460
What it essentially
does is it has

00:23:38.460 --> 00:23:42.870
a topic which all its devices
can publish do, either directly

00:23:42.870 --> 00:23:44.460
or via gateway.

00:23:44.460 --> 00:23:48.180
And you can have other services
to subscribe to these topics

00:23:48.180 --> 00:23:49.500
to ingest to consume the data.

00:23:53.140 --> 00:23:59.810
The three key things which
is unique to Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:23:59.810 --> 00:24:03.320
Number one is the durable
message persistence.

00:24:03.320 --> 00:24:04.340
What does it mean?

00:24:04.340 --> 00:24:07.970
So if you think about these
small constrained devices,

00:24:07.970 --> 00:24:09.770
they don't have
enough compute power

00:24:09.770 --> 00:24:14.690
to try to resend the data when
it's not getting an ack back

00:24:14.690 --> 00:24:18.020
from the ingestion service.

00:24:18.020 --> 00:24:19.760
You want to be able
to send the data out

00:24:19.760 --> 00:24:23.640
without much challenge, and
without much computing power.

00:24:23.640 --> 00:24:29.540
So Cloud pops up, makes
sure that the data is stored

00:24:29.540 --> 00:24:36.560
and we didn't send an ack
back until the published

00:24:36.560 --> 00:24:38.240
from the device is consumed.

00:24:38.240 --> 00:24:40.770
So that's one key advantage.

00:24:40.770 --> 00:24:44.060
A second is even though
your devices might

00:24:44.060 --> 00:24:47.060
be globally
distributed, you would

00:24:47.060 --> 00:24:49.910
want to be able to consume
it from a central location

00:24:49.910 --> 00:24:52.910
so you can remotely
monitor all those devices.

00:24:52.910 --> 00:24:56.450
So Cloud Pub/Sub is
a global service.

00:24:56.450 --> 00:25:00.710
And you don't have to think
about creating separate shards,

00:25:00.710 --> 00:25:02.120
or different regions.

00:25:02.120 --> 00:25:07.000
You just publish your devices
to one topic, and it handles it,

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:08.240
and it scales.

00:25:08.240 --> 00:25:13.550
In fact, Pub/Sub scales
internally to the extent

00:25:13.550 --> 00:25:17.240
that whether it is a couple
of thousands of messages

00:25:17.240 --> 00:25:22.150
or a couple of millions, it
just scales automatically.

00:25:22.150 --> 00:25:24.380
When it comes to
internet of things,

00:25:24.380 --> 00:25:26.090
there are certain
unpredictability

00:25:26.090 --> 00:25:27.540
in terms of load.

00:25:27.540 --> 00:25:30.140
So sometimes the devices
might be sending few data,

00:25:30.140 --> 00:25:32.750
and all of a sudden if there
is in real world there's

00:25:32.750 --> 00:25:35.240
increasing load, it might
be sending a massive amount

00:25:35.240 --> 00:25:37.460
of data for ingestion.

00:25:37.460 --> 00:25:40.010
And Cloud Pub/Sub
scales automatically

00:25:40.010 --> 00:25:44.210
without you needing to provision
or add any new infrastructure.

00:25:44.210 --> 00:25:48.290
In fact, it can handle anywhere
from a few thousand messages

00:25:48.290 --> 00:25:51.860
to millions of messages per
second in a matter of minutes

00:25:51.860 --> 00:25:53.960
without you needing
to do anything.

00:25:53.960 --> 00:25:56.650
So that's great about Pub/Sub.

00:25:56.650 --> 00:25:58.140
Another thing it
does well it is it

00:25:58.140 --> 00:26:02.180
separates the
downstream application

00:26:02.180 --> 00:26:04.920
from the upstream
deployment of your devices.

00:26:04.920 --> 00:26:08.380
So you can continue to
make changes or build

00:26:08.380 --> 00:26:13.400
new application which consumes
the data sent by your devices,

00:26:13.400 --> 00:26:16.250
or you can send it to a
different e-tail pipeline,

00:26:16.250 --> 00:26:20.840
you can have data flow or
cloud function consume it.

00:26:20.840 --> 00:26:24.290
And you can keep making changes
without needing to change

00:26:24.290 --> 00:26:25.580
your device deployment.

00:26:25.580 --> 00:26:28.670
So it creates an abstraction
between your upstream device

00:26:28.670 --> 00:26:31.530
deployed and your
downstream application.

00:26:31.530 --> 00:26:35.510
So it allows you to
iterate on your application

00:26:35.510 --> 00:26:38.480
at a much faster speed.

00:26:38.480 --> 00:26:41.020
And finally, it also
allows you to connect

00:26:41.020 --> 00:26:44.860
to some of the powerful
real time services

00:26:44.860 --> 00:26:47.072
we have, such as Cloud
Dataflow and Cloud Function,

00:26:47.072 --> 00:26:48.280
which I will just talk about.

00:26:51.040 --> 00:26:55.310
So now that you've ingested
this massive amount of data

00:26:55.310 --> 00:27:00.480
at scale, in case of
IoT application, most

00:27:00.480 --> 00:27:02.430
of the typical
time you would want

00:27:02.430 --> 00:27:05.340
to get some real time insights.

00:27:05.340 --> 00:27:07.300
Real time information.

00:27:07.300 --> 00:27:13.870
If you think about the
parking lot example,

00:27:13.870 --> 00:27:16.330
you would want to know real
time how many spaces are

00:27:16.330 --> 00:27:19.210
available near Moscone
Center, or how many spaces are

00:27:19.210 --> 00:27:22.960
available near a park
public garage space nearby.

00:27:22.960 --> 00:27:25.170
So that as you are
driving in, your car

00:27:25.170 --> 00:27:28.310
knows exactly which
parking garage to get into.

00:27:28.310 --> 00:27:32.260
And so Dataflow
has the mechanism

00:27:32.260 --> 00:27:37.690
where it can real time
process streams of data.

00:27:37.690 --> 00:27:41.200
You can create windows
to define boundaries

00:27:41.200 --> 00:27:47.170
and then it can aggregate,
you can run some aggregation

00:27:47.170 --> 00:27:51.650
or average, or run other
computation, as well.

00:27:51.650 --> 00:27:55.360
So Dataflow can be used
for a couple of things

00:27:55.360 --> 00:27:58.480
when you get streams
of data from devices.

00:27:58.480 --> 00:28:05.890
One is, as you get data from
different devices in the field,

00:28:05.890 --> 00:28:08.080
the data format
could be different.

00:28:08.080 --> 00:28:12.580
And what you would want to do
before you process or analyze

00:28:12.580 --> 00:28:14.650
the data is you would
want to standardize

00:28:14.650 --> 00:28:18.040
the format across this
different class of devices.

00:28:18.040 --> 00:28:19.930
And so you could use
that with Dataflow,

00:28:19.930 --> 00:28:23.680
where you can create
a real time job

00:28:23.680 --> 00:28:26.860
to do format translation
for data coming

00:28:26.860 --> 00:28:29.710
across different devices.

00:28:29.710 --> 00:28:33.520
Second is in real time,
it can create average.

00:28:33.520 --> 00:28:35.440
You can compute
an average, or you

00:28:35.440 --> 00:28:38.670
can do different transformation
on the data itself.

00:28:38.670 --> 00:28:41.950
In our simple example
for parking space,

00:28:41.950 --> 00:28:44.830
one could compute
average number of spaces

00:28:44.830 --> 00:28:48.280
available in a parking garage
or in the whole region,

00:28:48.280 --> 00:28:52.000
and then you can use that to
build some demand prediction,

00:28:52.000 --> 00:28:54.640
or even do pricing based
on demand, as well.

00:28:57.380 --> 00:28:59.500
And finally,
Dataflow can also be

00:28:59.500 --> 00:29:04.450
used to not just
work on the data

00:29:04.450 --> 00:29:07.030
which you're receiving from
the internet of things,

00:29:07.030 --> 00:29:10.450
you can also add data
from other services.

00:29:10.450 --> 00:29:13.600
So you can get weather data,
you can get some traffic data,

00:29:13.600 --> 00:29:17.370
and combine all this information
to do more rich analysis

00:29:17.370 --> 00:29:18.880
real time.

00:29:18.880 --> 00:29:20.620
That's the power
of data flow, which

00:29:20.620 --> 00:29:26.730
we think is great to
build IoT application.

00:29:26.730 --> 00:29:30.840
Once you process the data,
you want a simple way

00:29:30.840 --> 00:29:34.260
of moving the data or
storing the data so

00:29:34.260 --> 00:29:40.590
that you can do subsequent
analysis on that data.

00:29:40.590 --> 00:29:44.280
And we recently announced
Dataflow Templates, which

00:29:44.280 --> 00:29:49.530
is great for easy data movement
between Pub/Sub to other cloud

00:29:49.530 --> 00:29:53.990
services, such as Bigtable
or BigQuery for storage,

00:29:53.990 --> 00:29:57.600
DCS for storage, or
even for a Cloud ML.

00:29:57.600 --> 00:30:00.930
The unique advantage
of Dataflow Template

00:30:00.930 --> 00:30:04.920
is that as a developer, you can
create your own environment.

00:30:04.920 --> 00:30:09.130
You can create a template to
connect data from Pub/Sub to,

00:30:09.130 --> 00:30:11.160
let's say, BigQuery
in this example.

00:30:11.160 --> 00:30:13.960
Compile it, and
then you save it.

00:30:13.960 --> 00:30:18.016
And then a non
developer operation user

00:30:18.016 --> 00:30:19.140
doesn't have to compile it.

00:30:19.140 --> 00:30:22.140
All they do is they'll open
the Google Cloud console

00:30:22.140 --> 00:30:25.200
as it's shown here, they
will select the template

00:30:25.200 --> 00:30:26.940
which just pops up to BigQuery.

00:30:26.940 --> 00:30:28.080
It's parametrized.

00:30:28.080 --> 00:30:32.330
So they will enter the
topic of the Pub/Sub

00:30:32.330 --> 00:30:35.250
on which the data from
these devices are published.

00:30:35.250 --> 00:30:37.500
They will select
the table BigQuery,

00:30:37.500 --> 00:30:39.140
and then just run the job.

00:30:39.140 --> 00:30:41.430
And in the background,
the template automatically

00:30:41.430 --> 00:30:44.880
spins up a job and
transfers your data

00:30:44.880 --> 00:30:49.530
as it comes in to the BigQuery
table which you mentioned.

00:30:49.530 --> 00:30:54.060
So really easy, and it helps you
not only for storage, but also

00:30:54.060 --> 00:30:55.130
to scale.

00:30:55.130 --> 00:30:57.810
So as a developer you
create templates once,

00:30:57.810 --> 00:30:59.250
and the operations
folks can then

00:30:59.250 --> 00:31:01.530
execute on those run
jobs on this template

00:31:01.530 --> 00:31:04.350
without needing to
compile every time.

00:31:04.350 --> 00:31:09.480
You can also use templates
for creating simple rules.

00:31:09.480 --> 00:31:11.700
So as a developer,
you can create

00:31:11.700 --> 00:31:15.810
a rule that says every time
the number of parking space

00:31:15.810 --> 00:31:18.240
goes below 10 in
a public garage,

00:31:18.240 --> 00:31:20.430
I want to send an
alert, or I want

00:31:20.430 --> 00:31:27.054
to send SMS message to
to these mobile phones.

00:31:27.054 --> 00:31:28.470
You can also create
an alert where

00:31:28.470 --> 00:31:30.011
you say every time
something happens,

00:31:30.011 --> 00:31:31.920
I want to send a
message to datastore.

00:31:31.920 --> 00:31:33.840
All of that can be
done using Templates.

00:31:33.840 --> 00:31:36.290
As a developer, you create,
you build the template,

00:31:36.290 --> 00:31:37.770
you code it, and you save it.

00:31:37.770 --> 00:31:40.450
And not non-developer
users can just

00:31:40.450 --> 00:31:43.380
execute the job without
needing to compile every time.

00:31:43.380 --> 00:31:49.190
So that works great for the
purpose of scale, as well.

00:31:49.190 --> 00:31:52.880
Now once you've analyzed
the data at real time,

00:31:52.880 --> 00:31:55.730
you have ingested the
data, you've analyzed it,

00:31:55.730 --> 00:32:00.920
there are a number of options to
store the data on Google Cloud.

00:32:00.920 --> 00:32:04.280
You can use Google
Cloud Storage if you

00:32:04.280 --> 00:32:05.960
don't care about the structure.

00:32:05.960 --> 00:32:09.670
You can use Cloud Datastore if
you have key value pair format

00:32:09.670 --> 00:32:11.780
and the data is more structured.

00:32:11.780 --> 00:32:15.200
You can even use Cloud
SQL for that matter.

00:32:15.200 --> 00:32:18.500
But there's one service
which I will highlight

00:32:18.500 --> 00:32:21.740
which applies very
well for the IoT use

00:32:21.740 --> 00:32:24.790
case, which is Bigtable.

00:32:24.790 --> 00:32:29.020
If you think about the
public parking example,

00:32:29.020 --> 00:32:33.010
there are use cases where the
public garage, the garage owner

00:32:33.010 --> 00:32:37.600
might want to do some demand
analysis to really understand

00:32:37.600 --> 00:32:40.570
what are some of the
peak times when I

00:32:40.570 --> 00:32:43.720
see a peak demand for parking?

00:32:43.720 --> 00:32:49.840
And he or she can also use it
to do predictive pricing based

00:32:49.840 --> 00:32:51.490
on demand.

00:32:51.490 --> 00:32:55.630
Best way to do that is to
do time series analysis

00:32:55.630 --> 00:32:58.870
on the data stream, which the
parking garage and I might

00:32:58.870 --> 00:33:01.630
be collecting from all
the parking spaces.

00:33:01.630 --> 00:33:03.760
And this is where
Bigtable shines.

00:33:03.760 --> 00:33:06.130
Because Bigtable
scales horizontally.

00:33:06.130 --> 00:33:09.640
Whether you're talking about
few thousands of writes

00:33:09.640 --> 00:33:13.660
per second or millions of writes
per second, Bigtable scales

00:33:13.660 --> 00:33:14.560
internally.

00:33:14.560 --> 00:33:16.270
And it's great for
time series analysis,

00:33:16.270 --> 00:33:21.110
because it's a column
based, NoSQL database.

00:33:21.110 --> 00:33:22.970
The challenge with
Bigtable is you

00:33:22.970 --> 00:33:27.920
have to be very careful in
how you design the schema when

00:33:27.920 --> 00:33:30.440
you're thinking about
time series analysis.

00:33:30.440 --> 00:33:33.680
But what's great is we
have open TSDB, which

00:33:33.680 --> 00:33:37.340
is an open source
we strongly support.

00:33:37.340 --> 00:33:42.320
And it abstracts a time
series schema on Bigtable.

00:33:42.320 --> 00:33:45.860
So if you want to do time series
analysis on a series of data

00:33:45.860 --> 00:33:49.970
coming from your IoT devices,
and your IoT solution requires

00:33:49.970 --> 00:33:53.829
predictive processing, you
can use open TSDB on Bigtable,

00:33:53.829 --> 00:33:56.120
and you should be up and
running and designing the time

00:33:56.120 --> 00:33:57.580
series in a matter of no time.

00:34:02.760 --> 00:34:05.700
Now you've ingested
the data, you've

00:34:05.700 --> 00:34:09.300
processed data real time,
you stored the data, as well.

00:34:09.300 --> 00:34:13.050
There's an aspect of
reporting and visualization

00:34:13.050 --> 00:34:14.760
which is also important
when you think

00:34:14.760 --> 00:34:17.699
about building IoT solution.

00:34:17.699 --> 00:34:22.500
We do have two services
which can help you

00:34:22.500 --> 00:34:25.409
build reports and dashboards.

00:34:25.409 --> 00:34:31.530
There's data and Data Studio
what Datalab is great for, more

00:34:31.530 --> 00:34:32.909
sophisticated analysis.

00:34:32.909 --> 00:34:36.150
If you want to get the data,
and if you have statisticians

00:34:36.150 --> 00:34:40.710
with PhD's who would really want
to go deeper into the data use

00:34:40.710 --> 00:34:43.770
R to create some regression
model, Datalab great.

00:34:43.770 --> 00:34:47.100
It has a notebook interface,
it leverages existing Jupiter

00:34:47.100 --> 00:34:51.360
modules, and is suitable for
any data science and houses,

00:34:51.360 --> 00:34:54.280
or even building machine
learning models as well.

00:34:54.280 --> 00:34:56.969
It is closely integrated
with BigQuery and Cloud ML,

00:34:56.969 --> 00:35:01.050
so it makes it really easy
for you to take that data

00:35:01.050 --> 00:35:07.130
and then build a cloud ML model
for more sophisticated analysis

00:35:07.130 --> 00:35:08.980
down the road.

00:35:08.980 --> 00:35:11.710
Data Studio, on the
other hand, is primarily

00:35:11.710 --> 00:35:14.200
targeted for business analysts.

00:35:14.200 --> 00:35:16.990
So if you want to create
a simple dashboard where

00:35:16.990 --> 00:35:20.380
you want to show all your
fleet or truck movements

00:35:20.380 --> 00:35:23.180
on a Google map,
Data Studio is great.

00:35:23.180 --> 00:35:26.080
And then you can use that to
constantly monitor and generate

00:35:26.080 --> 00:35:29.090
report on an ongoing basis.

00:35:29.090 --> 00:35:31.490
It doesn't require any
technical expertise.

00:35:31.490 --> 00:35:35.840
You can drag, drop BigQuery
table or Excel sheet,

00:35:35.840 --> 00:35:40.520
or some other source, even
CSVs, onto Data Studio.

00:35:40.520 --> 00:35:43.610
And you can create some
really enriched dashboard

00:35:43.610 --> 00:35:46.260
for your reporting purposes.

00:35:46.260 --> 00:35:50.390
So Datalab and Data Studio is
great for generating reports

00:35:50.390 --> 00:35:53.450
to really tease out actionable
insights from the data

00:35:53.450 --> 00:35:55.380
after you've processed
and stored it.

00:35:58.790 --> 00:36:00.540
So I talked about the
different processes.

00:36:00.540 --> 00:36:04.090
I'm sure you would want
to see all this in action.

00:36:04.090 --> 00:36:07.200
So I'm going invite
Jose Ugia, who's

00:36:07.200 --> 00:36:09.415
going to give a demo of
a smart fleet which they

00:36:09.415 --> 00:36:10.540
have built on Google Cloud.

00:36:10.540 --> 00:36:11.040
Jose.

00:36:17.332 --> 00:36:18.940
JOSE UGIA: Thank, Indranil.

00:36:18.940 --> 00:36:23.420
So every time I see the
slide that Indranil just

00:36:23.420 --> 00:36:35.950
showed about the respected
growth of the IoT devices

00:36:35.950 --> 00:36:38.020
in the industry,
then I always get

00:36:38.020 --> 00:36:41.050
to this profound,
yet simple thought.

00:36:41.050 --> 00:36:43.770
And that is that we'll
be surrounded by them.

00:36:43.770 --> 00:36:46.810
And because of that, in tech
we have gotten things wrong

00:36:46.810 --> 00:36:49.630
a few times, especially
when it comes to security,

00:36:49.630 --> 00:36:52.880
when it comes to scalability,
when it comes to reliability.

00:36:52.880 --> 00:36:54.430
And I think this
time what we can do

00:36:54.430 --> 00:36:57.350
is try to learn from
the previous mistakes.

00:36:57.350 --> 00:37:00.260
Come up with a set of
practices in all those things.

00:37:00.260 --> 00:37:03.500
So by the time this happens,
we are ready to do that.

00:37:03.500 --> 00:37:06.310
And that's something that in our
company we have really present,

00:37:06.310 --> 00:37:12.050
and we love to keep it that
way for the months to come.

00:37:12.050 --> 00:37:16.750
So at Noa, what we
do is, our vision

00:37:16.750 --> 00:37:19.660
is to transform the
way we understand

00:37:19.660 --> 00:37:22.050
and utilize urban
transportation.

00:37:22.050 --> 00:37:24.020
And we do this in two main ways.

00:37:24.020 --> 00:37:26.890
We want to do that in
two main verticals.

00:37:26.890 --> 00:37:31.710
Which are making transportation
more convenient to you

00:37:31.710 --> 00:37:32.880
within urban fleets--

00:37:32.880 --> 00:37:38.180
that's commuters, that students,
that in police of campuses.

00:37:38.180 --> 00:37:40.780
We want to make
it easier to find

00:37:40.780 --> 00:37:43.739
actual means of
transportation to move around.

00:37:43.739 --> 00:37:45.280
But the most important
part, and this

00:37:45.280 --> 00:37:47.770
is where we are putting
most of our efforts,

00:37:47.770 --> 00:37:52.990
is on reducing the operational
costs of managing those fleets.

00:37:52.990 --> 00:37:56.410
Especially when it comes
to a large number of them.

00:37:56.410 --> 00:37:59.050
So there are things
involved that

00:37:59.050 --> 00:38:01.690
grow with the number
of devices linearly.

00:38:01.690 --> 00:38:04.040
And what we want
through automation

00:38:04.040 --> 00:38:08.050
and add in features that
provide insights and knowledge,

00:38:08.050 --> 00:38:11.230
we want to reduce this cost.

00:38:11.230 --> 00:38:14.280
And so one of the companies
that we work with is Google.

00:38:14.280 --> 00:38:17.710
This is why we have
this bike over there.

00:38:17.710 --> 00:38:20.080
This bike has
something here which

00:38:20.080 --> 00:38:23.740
we call the donut, which
is our hardware device that

00:38:23.740 --> 00:38:24.970
helps us sense.

00:38:24.970 --> 00:38:27.850
So next time you go
to the Google campus

00:38:27.850 --> 00:38:29.980
and you think about taking
one of those bicycles

00:38:29.980 --> 00:38:32.650
as a souvenir, you
may think twice,

00:38:32.650 --> 00:38:34.600
because we are
tracking those things.

00:38:34.600 --> 00:38:38.740
So if we can switch
quickly to the wall,

00:38:38.740 --> 00:38:40.440
that sounds very powerful.

00:38:40.440 --> 00:38:43.340
Switch to the walls.

00:38:43.340 --> 00:38:45.490
This is how the
device looks like.

00:38:45.490 --> 00:38:50.390
So as you can see, it's
a small rounded element

00:38:50.390 --> 00:38:52.700
with machinery inside.

00:38:52.700 --> 00:38:55.620
And you may imagine
why we call it donut,

00:38:55.620 --> 00:38:57.050
that's obvious reasons.

00:38:57.050 --> 00:39:00.470
And it has this shape because
it goes next to the wheel.

00:39:00.470 --> 00:39:02.540
So the interesting thing
is that you can simply

00:39:02.540 --> 00:39:06.620
take that wheel out, replace it
with one that has this device,

00:39:06.620 --> 00:39:08.930
and then boom, you're connected.

00:39:08.930 --> 00:39:11.840
As easy as that.

00:39:11.840 --> 00:39:13.810
What this device
senses is things

00:39:13.810 --> 00:39:19.270
like humidity, temperature, gas,
or pollution related particle.

00:39:19.270 --> 00:39:21.100
And we also have
antennas to connect

00:39:21.100 --> 00:39:22.900
to the cellular network.

00:39:22.900 --> 00:39:24.460
We know the location
of the device,

00:39:24.460 --> 00:39:26.750
because we have GPS
antennas as well.

00:39:26.750 --> 00:39:31.100
All together to provide us with
the information that we need.

00:39:31.100 --> 00:39:34.210
And if we can go back
to the slides please,

00:39:34.210 --> 00:39:37.390
the information that we
need to help these operators

00:39:37.390 --> 00:39:40.287
make those decisions that
make them reduce costs.

00:39:40.287 --> 00:39:41.620
There is already cost reduction.

00:39:41.620 --> 00:39:44.350
But in some cases,
this means viability.

00:39:44.350 --> 00:39:47.170
It may mean the
difference between having

00:39:47.170 --> 00:39:48.270
a program or not.

00:39:48.270 --> 00:39:53.140
So as you may have heard, there
are a few cities in the US

00:39:53.140 --> 00:39:55.450
in which this has really
been a huge struggle.

00:39:55.450 --> 00:39:58.240
In which the public systems
cannot sustain those programs

00:39:58.240 --> 00:40:00.550
anymore because
of their pricing.

00:40:00.550 --> 00:40:04.080
So in some cases that will
mean having them or not.

00:40:04.080 --> 00:40:07.210
And this is what really
makes us excited about it.

00:40:07.210 --> 00:40:10.840
Before we jump into matter,
we have been running on Google

00:40:10.840 --> 00:40:13.720
for a few months now.

00:40:13.720 --> 00:40:16.690
And I thought it was worth
sharing some of the stats

00:40:16.690 --> 00:40:19.340
that we have gathered with you.

00:40:19.340 --> 00:40:22.710
Googlers, they seem
to be healthy people.

00:40:22.710 --> 00:40:27.320
They cycle the world every
month with their bicycles.

00:40:27.320 --> 00:40:31.340
Everyone knows the diameter of
our lovely blue planet, right?

00:40:31.340 --> 00:40:33.340
So that's a lot of distance.

00:40:33.340 --> 00:40:35.937
And we only have a subset
of the devices equipped,

00:40:35.937 --> 00:40:37.020
we don't have all of them.

00:40:37.020 --> 00:40:39.680
So that's something
that came as a surprise,

00:40:39.680 --> 00:40:41.994
not because we thought that
everyone was sedentary,

00:40:41.994 --> 00:40:44.410
but because we thought the
activity was going to be lower.

00:40:44.410 --> 00:40:45.890
It was really great to see that.

00:40:45.890 --> 00:40:50.840
We have more than 100,000 trips
every three months at Google,

00:40:50.840 --> 00:40:53.180
and there might be
something going on at night.

00:40:53.180 --> 00:40:57.590
Because we have seen that
after 8 PM And before 6 AM,

00:40:57.590 --> 00:40:59.580
there are 1,000
trips every month.

00:40:59.580 --> 00:41:02.530
So not sure if we
will find out or not,

00:41:02.530 --> 00:41:04.880
but there's some kind of
secret project or fund

00:41:04.880 --> 00:41:07.580
that happens really late at
night within the Google campus.

00:41:10.300 --> 00:41:12.490
But how do we leverage
Google products?

00:41:12.490 --> 00:41:16.060
And why this is
really useful for us.

00:41:16.060 --> 00:41:20.000
We base our data
pipeline from our devices

00:41:20.000 --> 00:41:21.490
in two main principles.

00:41:21.490 --> 00:41:22.662
One of them is real time.

00:41:22.662 --> 00:41:25.120
We need to leave in real time
because there are many things

00:41:25.120 --> 00:41:26.035
that depend on this.

00:41:26.035 --> 00:41:28.900
Like security, like
infrastructure,

00:41:28.900 --> 00:41:32.300
like availability, like
the month of bicycles.

00:41:32.300 --> 00:41:34.850
And we need to know about
those as quickly as possible.

00:41:34.850 --> 00:41:38.560
If someone is maybe trying to
steal a bike, any of you maybe,

00:41:38.560 --> 00:41:41.320
we need to know but as
quickly as possible.

00:41:41.320 --> 00:41:44.890
So we want that to be
milliseconds or seconds.

00:41:44.890 --> 00:41:47.270
That's why we
ingest into Pub/Sub.

00:41:47.270 --> 00:41:49.830
And once we do that,
we transfer in parallel

00:41:49.830 --> 00:41:51.280
to all the different services.

00:41:51.280 --> 00:41:55.390
That is Alert, that is location
updates, that is insights,

00:41:55.390 --> 00:41:57.170
as well.

00:41:57.170 --> 00:42:00.220
The other principle that we
have is that our information

00:42:00.220 --> 00:42:03.310
is simple at the
beginning, how we want

00:42:03.310 --> 00:42:04.910
to transform that as it comes.

00:42:04.910 --> 00:42:06.970
So think of it as
LEGO pieces, right?

00:42:06.970 --> 00:42:10.240
By themselves, they may be just
colorful, beautiful pieces,

00:42:10.240 --> 00:42:12.680
but they mean almost nothing.

00:42:12.680 --> 00:42:14.680
What we do with them,
when we put them together,

00:42:14.680 --> 00:42:16.805
when we assemble them, then
we can construct things

00:42:16.805 --> 00:42:20.470
like cars, rockets,
houses, buildings, things

00:42:20.470 --> 00:42:21.580
that have meaning, right?

00:42:21.580 --> 00:42:23.330
So this is what we do
with our information

00:42:23.330 --> 00:42:25.210
as soon as it comes,
and we transform it

00:42:25.210 --> 00:42:28.360
as it arrives and then send it
to the different services which

00:42:28.360 --> 00:42:29.740
are interested.

00:42:29.740 --> 00:42:31.430
And that is once
again within the order

00:42:31.430 --> 00:42:33.355
of milliseconds or seconds.

00:42:36.100 --> 00:42:37.730
This is how it looks
when we translate

00:42:37.730 --> 00:42:41.900
what I have said into Google
services, or Google products

00:42:41.900 --> 00:42:43.160
from a really high level.

00:42:43.160 --> 00:42:45.160
There are two things to
highlight in this slide.

00:42:45.160 --> 00:42:49.520
One of them is that we use
MQTT, we have a set of machines

00:42:49.520 --> 00:42:52.860
that we use as a gateway to
which our devices communicate.

00:42:52.860 --> 00:42:53.560
They ingest.

00:42:53.560 --> 00:42:56.180
We use MQTT as a protocol,
and protocol buffers

00:42:56.180 --> 00:42:57.164
as a message format.

00:42:57.164 --> 00:42:59.330
Just because it's convenient,
we are in the outside,

00:42:59.330 --> 00:43:01.940
we're in a hostile
environment with rain,

00:43:01.940 --> 00:43:05.300
with cold, with wind, with
reduced cellular connectivity,

00:43:05.300 --> 00:43:08.060
with reduced GPS coverage.

00:43:08.060 --> 00:43:12.860
And so having the chance to
have a protocol that is meant

00:43:12.860 --> 00:43:15.440
thought for that is great,
that's why we use it.

00:43:15.440 --> 00:43:19.190
The other one is that if you
see the second part, this spurt

00:43:19.190 --> 00:43:22.940
of Pub/Sub plus Data
Flow, this layers which

00:43:22.940 --> 00:43:26.090
seem to be repeated, this tax
is what I just mentioned before.

00:43:26.090 --> 00:43:29.150
This allows us to, once
we ingest into Pub/Sub

00:43:29.150 --> 00:43:32.540
and we process in Data Flow,
then some of those results

00:43:32.540 --> 00:43:35.720
will make it into storages,
or into different services

00:43:35.720 --> 00:43:36.350
themselves.

00:43:36.350 --> 00:43:38.570
Some of those will be
republished into Pub/Sub

00:43:38.570 --> 00:43:41.390
so we can keep on transforming
and analyzing that.

00:43:41.390 --> 00:43:46.280
And I'll show you a couple of
examples of that in a second.

00:43:46.280 --> 00:43:49.490
Lastly we'll love to
touch on something

00:43:49.490 --> 00:43:53.120
that has really changed the
way we analyze our information.

00:43:53.120 --> 00:43:56.150
We used to spend a lot
of time to find out

00:43:56.150 --> 00:43:57.550
how our data was performing.

00:43:57.550 --> 00:44:01.160
That is in terms of insights, in
terms of business intelligence,

00:44:01.160 --> 00:44:02.270
but also monitoring.

00:44:02.270 --> 00:44:04.228
Because we have our
[INAUDIBLE] devices, and we

00:44:04.228 --> 00:44:05.200
have data about those.

00:44:05.200 --> 00:44:07.970
So we can find out
whether the GPS accuracy

00:44:07.970 --> 00:44:09.500
is good or bad, for example.

00:44:09.500 --> 00:44:12.200
We take a longer
time, now we leverage

00:44:12.200 --> 00:44:15.710
where we make use of BigQuery
query so we can make decisions

00:44:15.710 --> 00:44:16.530
much faster.

00:44:16.530 --> 00:44:20.420
And sometimes this really
provides for a big difference

00:44:20.420 --> 00:44:23.330
in how our product
is being run, and how

00:44:23.330 --> 00:44:26.750
to invest the little resources
that we have as a small company

00:44:26.750 --> 00:44:30.630
to make the best out of
it for our customers.

00:44:30.630 --> 00:44:34.220
If we can switch to the product
demo without further ado,

00:44:34.220 --> 00:44:35.510
the theory was great.

00:44:35.510 --> 00:44:38.940
But things are much better
than when we show them live.

00:44:43.280 --> 00:44:47.455
And so let's all invoke
all the demo gods,

00:44:47.455 --> 00:44:51.700
and let's challenge them
right here on the stage.

00:44:51.700 --> 00:44:52.960
Can we switch now?

00:44:52.960 --> 00:44:54.920
Yeah.

00:44:54.920 --> 00:44:58.070
So what you will see
here is just a reflection

00:44:58.070 --> 00:45:01.730
of what I just explained.

00:45:01.730 --> 00:45:04.560
In this case, I want
to show you two things.

00:45:04.560 --> 00:45:09.880
One is right where we are
right now where this device is,

00:45:09.880 --> 00:45:15.240
and where one of our
colleagues is cycling.

00:45:15.240 --> 00:45:18.910
She must have gone
really far, there she is.

00:45:18.910 --> 00:45:21.213
The reason why it's green
is because she's moving.

00:45:21.213 --> 00:45:26.220
And this shows a reflection
of the current snapshot.

00:45:26.220 --> 00:45:27.800
This is where she
is right now, this

00:45:27.800 --> 00:45:29.710
is the battery the
device has, this

00:45:29.710 --> 00:45:31.620
is the status of the device.

00:45:31.620 --> 00:45:35.610
You saw that I just moved this
device that we have over here.

00:45:35.610 --> 00:45:39.540
In a few seconds,
if I don't break it,

00:45:39.540 --> 00:45:41.130
it's going to get
green [INAUDIBLE].

00:45:41.130 --> 00:45:44.100
So this is just transmitting
from here right now real time.

00:45:44.100 --> 00:45:47.170
And you saw that quickly,
we saw it on the system.

00:45:47.170 --> 00:45:51.300
But things get more interesting
if we go to the Google Fleet.

00:45:51.300 --> 00:45:52.800
Because you just
saw three bicycles.

00:45:52.800 --> 00:45:56.430
But if we go in here,
there's plenty more.

00:45:56.430 --> 00:45:58.020
This is right now
in Mountain View

00:45:58.020 --> 00:46:02.470
after lunchtime, which is one
of the peak times of activity.

00:46:02.470 --> 00:46:05.100
So if we get to one of the
most crowded areas in here,

00:46:05.100 --> 00:46:08.142
in the cross with the
Shorebird and Charleston,

00:46:08.142 --> 00:46:09.350
then we have bicycles moving.

00:46:09.350 --> 00:46:11.180
I don't know if
you can see them.

00:46:11.180 --> 00:46:12.620
But as we move
through the screen,

00:46:12.620 --> 00:46:15.210
they are moving right now.

00:46:15.210 --> 00:46:17.130
So this is just
the basic snapshot.

00:46:17.130 --> 00:46:20.490
But why do we reaggregate
and process information?

00:46:20.490 --> 00:46:22.350
What can we get
out of it, right?

00:46:22.350 --> 00:46:26.030
If we go to Trips, which is one
of the most interesting views

00:46:26.030 --> 00:46:30.480
for many of our operators,
just with one query

00:46:30.480 --> 00:46:32.910
we can see what's the
path of that bicycle.

00:46:32.910 --> 00:46:37.470
If we want to learn about what
happened to specific bicycle,

00:46:37.470 --> 00:46:39.580
one of the back, or
it may be suffering

00:46:39.580 --> 00:46:41.320
any suspicious behavior.

00:46:41.320 --> 00:46:43.465
We can come here, now
it's not trackings,

00:46:43.465 --> 00:46:44.840
anymore it's not
little messages,

00:46:44.840 --> 00:46:45.880
it's not the LEGO pieces.

00:46:45.880 --> 00:46:47.005
Now we have that full trip.

00:46:47.005 --> 00:46:50.490
So we have the start, the end,
the velocity, the distance.

00:46:50.490 --> 00:46:53.030
Things that make it much
easier for us to consume.

00:46:53.030 --> 00:46:56.240
And then here we can keep
going back as we can see.

00:46:56.240 --> 00:46:59.760
And we'll go through the
history of that bicycle.

00:46:59.760 --> 00:47:06.560
Concentrate on the trips
that we are interested in.

00:47:06.560 --> 00:47:11.720
Finally, we have seen
trackings, you have seen trips.

00:47:11.720 --> 00:47:13.140
This is great.

00:47:13.140 --> 00:47:15.740
The interesting part
of this approach

00:47:15.740 --> 00:47:19.190
is that we can go from
here into whatever we need.

00:47:19.190 --> 00:47:22.240
So for example, once we have
Trips, we do two things.

00:47:22.240 --> 00:47:26.060
We run a Data Flow job
to filter them out.

00:47:26.060 --> 00:47:27.780
Because you know,
GPS has errors,

00:47:27.780 --> 00:47:29.090
so you need to filter that out.

00:47:29.090 --> 00:47:32.060
So you need to maybe
go through certain APIs

00:47:32.060 --> 00:47:33.270
to make it a bit clearer.

00:47:33.270 --> 00:47:34.670
So we do that, but
another thing that we do

00:47:34.670 --> 00:47:36.650
is we calculate metrics,
organization-wise.

00:47:36.650 --> 00:47:39.320
So what you're seeing
is the aggregate metrics

00:47:39.320 --> 00:47:44.400
for distance in Google for
the overall organization.

00:47:44.400 --> 00:47:47.220
So there's a time
based serious metric.

00:47:47.220 --> 00:47:53.900
And as you can see, as we
change the actual date range,

00:47:53.900 --> 00:47:56.640
this is really quick, this
is round trip, by the way.

00:47:56.640 --> 00:47:59.390
The servers are in Ireland,
so it's round trip.

00:47:59.390 --> 00:48:00.560
You see that's really quick.

00:48:00.560 --> 00:48:02.018
But that's not the
important thing.

00:48:02.018 --> 00:48:04.640
The important thing is
that this information

00:48:04.640 --> 00:48:05.900
is really pre-calculated.

00:48:05.900 --> 00:48:07.484
That's why it's quick.

00:48:07.484 --> 00:48:08.900
Which means that
our servers don't

00:48:08.900 --> 00:48:11.390
have to calculate
it every single time

00:48:11.390 --> 00:48:12.810
that we run this query.

00:48:16.330 --> 00:48:19.150
Not only that, but also that we
can use all those things which

00:48:19.150 --> 00:48:22.390
had already denormalized
and stored in our system,

00:48:22.390 --> 00:48:25.510
in our databases, we can use
them then for further jobs.

00:48:25.510 --> 00:48:27.740
Like analytics,
machine learning,

00:48:27.740 --> 00:48:30.310
and so on and so forth.

00:48:30.310 --> 00:48:33.430
And so with that, this is
what I want to show you.

00:48:33.430 --> 00:48:37.690
I hope this resembles something
that you may be thinking of,

00:48:37.690 --> 00:48:39.730
some project that
you may have already.

00:48:39.730 --> 00:48:44.090
And I hope this is good insights
for you for the actual months

00:48:44.090 --> 00:48:44.590
to come.

00:48:44.590 --> 00:48:46.420
If you want to
find out more, you

00:48:46.420 --> 00:48:50.080
can find us under
they're [INAUDIBLE].

00:48:50.080 --> 00:48:51.700
There's a little
section on the right

00:48:51.700 --> 00:48:54.600
that says something like jobs
you may be interested in.

00:48:54.600 --> 00:48:57.520
But other than that,
I want to thank you,

00:48:57.520 --> 00:48:59.496
and would love to hand
it back to Indranil.

00:49:09.247 --> 00:49:10.830
INDRANIL CHAKRABORTY:
Thank you, Jose.

00:49:10.830 --> 00:49:17.480
So as you saw in Jose's demo,
he used Google Cloud not just

00:49:17.480 --> 00:49:23.910
to track real time the
different bicycles,

00:49:23.910 --> 00:49:27.330
but he also used it
to do post-analysis,

00:49:27.330 --> 00:49:30.450
and even those analyses
were pretty fast, because he

00:49:30.450 --> 00:49:32.010
was using BigQuery and others.

00:49:35.550 --> 00:49:38.900
And it's interesting that this
whole application can also

00:49:38.900 --> 00:49:41.300
be used for urban
transportation,

00:49:41.300 --> 00:49:44.840
and even in the case
of parking lot space.

00:49:44.840 --> 00:49:50.420
We also do have a demo on the
third floor in the IoT expert

00:49:50.420 --> 00:49:51.890
booth.

00:49:51.890 --> 00:49:54.440
I would highly encourage that
you guys should check it out.

00:49:54.440 --> 00:49:59.360
Essentially what it does is it
shows the simple application

00:49:59.360 --> 00:50:03.990
of Pub/Sub, Data Flow, and
is compatible with Android

00:50:03.990 --> 00:50:05.160
Things, as well.

00:50:05.160 --> 00:50:08.470
So what does demo does, and
as you go, you will find out.

00:50:08.470 --> 00:50:14.640
It has a couple of temperature
sensors on Raspberry Pi.

00:50:14.640 --> 00:50:17.060
And these thermometer
sensors are

00:50:17.060 --> 00:50:19.730
sending temperature
measurement data,

00:50:19.730 --> 00:50:21.530
continuously sending
the measurement data,

00:50:21.530 --> 00:50:23.960
to Pub/Sub, which
then distributes

00:50:23.960 --> 00:50:25.730
the data to Data Flow.

00:50:25.730 --> 00:50:28.580
And data flow does
in real time compute

00:50:28.580 --> 00:50:30.050
the average temperature.

00:50:30.050 --> 00:50:32.470
And then it goes through
different pipeline.

00:50:32.470 --> 00:50:35.480
And every time, as the
average temperature

00:50:35.480 --> 00:50:37.790
reaches above a
certain threshold,

00:50:37.790 --> 00:50:41.060
it fires a Firebase
message to the controller

00:50:41.060 --> 00:50:42.500
which turns on the fan.

00:50:42.500 --> 00:50:45.710
So it's a very
simple, nifty demo.

00:50:45.710 --> 00:50:48.440
But it shows you
the different pieces

00:50:48.440 --> 00:50:50.090
in a very simplistic way.

00:50:50.090 --> 00:50:54.170
And how you can use Pub/Sub,
Data Flow, Android Things,

00:50:54.170 --> 00:50:57.560
and other cloud services
to build your IoT solution

00:50:57.560 --> 00:51:00.340
for your specific use case.

